  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "pvaHx5xlNTFGJgjZNxeYiI2q4RI=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:49.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The revision-history panel is rendered by Connect, outside the editor's component tree \u2014 which is why it can only be *styled* through scoped CSS. Its *visibility*, though, is ordinary global state with a three-function API:\n\n```ts\nfunction useRevisionHistoryVisible(): boolean | undefined;\nfunction showRevisionHistory(): void;\nfunction hideRevisionHistory(): void;\n```\n\n`showRevisionHistory` and `hideRevisionHistory` are plain functions rather than hooks, so they are safe to call from a click handler, an effect, or a keyboard shortcut. Neither takes arguments: the panel always targets the currently selected document, so there is no way to open history for some other document from here.\n\n`useRevisionHistoryVisible` returns `boolean | undefined`, and the third state is meaningful \u2014 `undefined` is \"not yet initialised\" rather than \"hidden\". Treat it as falsy for rendering, but do not write `visible === false` as your hidden check.\n\nThis matters because the toolbar `history` button is not the only sensible affordance. An editor that wants a \"see what changed\" link inside its own layout, a drive app that opens history on a keyboard shortcut, or an editor that wants to close the panel when the user starts editing again, can all drive the panel directly. The reverse read is equally useful: an editor can consult `useRevisionHistoryVisible()` to collapse a sidebar or suppress its own controls while history is on screen, since the panel takes over the view.\n\nCombined with `disabledControls={[\"history\"]}` on `DocumentToolbar`, these hooks let you remove the built-in button entirely and replace it with your own trigger without losing the feature.\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/05-EditorsUI/01-ReactHooks.md (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:35:49.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:49.000Z"
    }
  },
